WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:07.800
The day before Thanksgiving,
we went to my nana's house,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.970
my dad's mom's house.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:14.310
And you know, I'm not gonna get
too sad, but I will say that

00:00:17.245 --> 00:00:20.453
it was difficult in one way particular

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.660
because it was the first
time Papa wasn't there.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.105
You know, having passed away months ago

00:00:27.105 --> 00:00:31.890
and with my uncle Dan also
passing away right before that,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:32.940
which I've talked about on the show.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.667
So it was like, there
was this anticipation of,

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:38.700
"Okay, there's gonna be some
empty seats, so to speak."

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.150
Now, it was never a plan
to leave seats empty,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.620
but actually what happened,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.210
what had happened was after our concert,

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.510
I've told you this, right?

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.750
Well, at our concert at the
North Carolina State Fair,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.750
my mom and Louis road there
with my dad and Nancy.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.300
So this is your parents

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.230
and their new partners
hanging out together?

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:09.063
Yeah.

00:01:09.063 --> 00:01:10.670
And nana all rode in dad's truck up there

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.350
because they were concerned about parking.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.280
But it has been 30 years or more than that

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.120
since your parents were a couple.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.600
So we've had a lot of time

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.160
for the awkwardness to
settle a little bit.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:29.160
Well, they have never ridden
in the same car anywhere.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:35.380
And so when dad and Nancy
offered to take them, I was like,

00:01:36.287 --> 00:01:37.640
"Okay, I'll let them know."

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.160
And then like, they did
it and it went great.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.070
And then come to find
out on the ride there,

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.050
the ride back there talking
about Thanksgiving plans

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.430
and how we're coming into
town for Thanksgiving,

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:46.984
now it's Christmas time.

00:01:46.984 --> 00:01:48.750
And they were like, "You know what?"

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:49.797
Dad and Nancy were like,

00:01:49.797 --> 00:01:53.807
"Sue, you and Louis should
come to our Thanksgiving."

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.167
And I'm hearing about this and I'm like,

00:01:58.167 --> 00:01:59.830
"After they had decided?"

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.830
They were like, "Well, we were invited."

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:06.437
I was like, "Sounds weird but great."

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:08.703
Okay.

00:02:08.703 --> 00:02:10.218
♪ We make the rules ♪

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:13.790
So, but to the first time ever,

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:18.790
my mom came to my dad's side
to like a big family event

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.320
on my dad's side of the family.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.540
But I will say, 'cause if-

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.110
Even birthday parties growing up,

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.020
I mean, they were all separate.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.520
I'd have twice as many birthday parties,

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:28.460
twice as many Christmases.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.710
What I'm getting at is.

00:02:30.623 --> 00:02:35.623
You know, this is, you know,
super common in modern society

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:40.300
for families to split up
and there'd be two entities.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.070
And then the children have
to kind of make choices

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:44.660
and go from place to place.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.120
It is pretty novel concept.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.680
If you can all get along
I mean, get together.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.680
It sounds like a movie
because it's probably is.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.910
Christmas Eve we'd always
go to nanny's house.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.940
My mom's mom, my dad's side family

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:57.830
and had him nothing to do with it

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.950
because Christmas night was nana's night

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.190
for my dad's side of the family.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:02.900
My mom had nothing to do with that.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.480
I was the only one that would do both.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:05.313
And it was great.

00:03:05.313 --> 00:03:06.180
'Cause I got, you know,

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.730
you end up getting a lot
more presence, I think.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:09.980
Well, yeah, definitely.

00:03:11.421 --> 00:03:13.330
But now I don't really get much presence

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:14.220
and this is Thanksgiving.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.260
So presents are out of the equation.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.190
Oh, your family doesn't
do Thanksgiving present?

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.750
No, they don't.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:21.250
We're not even thankful.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:22.083
Oh.

00:03:22.083 --> 00:03:23.534
Just kidding.

00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:25.230
We don't even do thankful.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:26.660
It was this interesting dynamic.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.150
And there's like, I
mean, I think there were

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.080
what was the number?

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.700
Ended up being like 16 people there.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.110
So it wasn't like an awkwardly
small group of people

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:36.210
sitting around a small table.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.103
It's still a very, we're putting
a bunch of tables together.

00:03:40.272 --> 00:03:43.850
It turned out to be, you
know, there were still moments

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.550
of it being very sad.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.210
You know, it's like really experiencing,

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:52.060
missing these key members of
our family for the first time.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:57.060
So it in no way erases that,
but it created something new.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:04.330
I mean, my cousin Kurt,
and they had a newborn,

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.410
you know, three months old,
first time we met the baby,

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.110
it was like, so there's all
this excitement that like,

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.590
gives something to celebrate, you know,

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:20.850
which was very much needed.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:24.540
And then I looked down
at that end of the table

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.190
and it was like, my mom's sitting there.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.770
It's like so weird but this is great.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.750
I mean, I think everybody's
having a good time

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:35.860
and you know, everybody
did have a good time.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:36.850
It all worked out.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.274
And at one point, you know,
I've talked about Louis,

00:04:40.274 --> 00:04:43.010
my stepdad, who I'll never call my stepdad

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.430
because of that's just weird.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:48.110
'Cause they got married
way after I got married,

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.638
but I'm like his catch
phrase,:Good, good, good."

00:04:51.638 --> 00:04:53.290
Told you about that, right?

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:54.123
Yes.

00:04:54.123 --> 00:04:55.720
We talked about it, we built it up,

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.111
but the kids were like,

00:04:57.111 --> 00:04:58.100
"All right, Papa Louis

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.030
is gonna be at the
Thanksgiving dinner tonight."

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:00.910
And they're like, "Tonight?"

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.720
And nana's like, "Yes." So-

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.131
Let's do a drinking game kids.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.593
Every time he's just, "Good, good, good,

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.093
you take a shot of gravy."

00:05:11.093 --> 00:05:12.803
That's a good idea.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.170
And yeah, I'm like, "Silly
you've got to listen for it."

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:19.810
And then lo and behold, the
kids get up from the table

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:21.650
after like we're all
sitting there at the table

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.100
after the food, after everybody's eaten,

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.810
some people start getting
up there doing stuff,

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:27.470
but most of the people still at the table.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:32.220
And then Louis is like,
"Oh boy, that was good."

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.313
My ears perk up.

00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:37.100
And I look across the
table at Christy's like-

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.330
Pulling a cord.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:40.450
Christy's eyes met mine.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:44.800
And he was like, "Oh gosh, that
was good. Good, good, good."

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.860
I was like, "Yes."

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.000
And then Christy and
I looked at each other

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.820
and that was like the best
moment of Thanksgiving.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:51.020
And then I look around for the kids

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:52.260
and they're not at a table.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:53.093
They didn't even witness it.

00:05:53.093 --> 00:05:54.150
I think Lily was at the table

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:55.930
and she didn't hear it somehow.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:06:00.550
And that was the only, good,
good, good of the whole-

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:01.383
You're kidding?

00:06:01.383 --> 00:06:02.220
That's the only one he did.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.042
And he gave you like a warning shot.

00:06:04.042 --> 00:06:04.875
Yeah.

00:06:04.875 --> 00:06:07.350
I mean, everybody could have
gathered around during that-

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.330
I got it, I just wanted
the kids to get it.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.620
I mean the next day, which
was Thanksgiving day,

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.300
we're at nanny's house.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:14.560
So my mom's side of the family,

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.500
my dad and Nancy weren't invited to that.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.520
I don't know, it didn't go both ways.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:19.710
I don't know what happened.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:20.813
Interesting.

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:21.940
And were there,

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.400
Louis didn't give a
good, good, good there.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.580
It was good, good, good.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.010
Did he have a bad, bad, bad Thanksgiving?

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:32.010
Well, it was great,
Thanksgiving was great there.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.159
It was like nanny and her
siblings, uncle Jimmy was there.

00:06:37.159 --> 00:06:38.951
Never seen uncle Jimmy.

00:06:38.951 --> 00:06:39.940
Yeah.

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:41.420
He was there.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.603
He's hard to understand.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:46.820
I love the way he talks.

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:48.460
It's just the, I'm just saying.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:49.440
It needs to be subtitled?

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:50.954
Yeah it needs to be.

00:06:50.954 --> 00:06:53.118
He needs to have his own
subtitle around his neck.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.477
He needs to have an iPad that
he wears that has subtitles.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:00.350
I'm not gonna do an impersonation

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:03.597
because I think that would
be cruel, but it's it...

00:07:03.597 --> 00:07:05.777
I love him for it.

00:07:05.777 --> 00:07:08.230
That's the thing about getting
together with your family

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:10.980
is just, yeah, I'm in
more observation mode,

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:12.360
you know, it's not like

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.350
we're getting into deep
conversations or anything.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:15.740
We're just sitting around.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:19.550
Nanny gets so cold that
she set that literally,

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:22.050
it was like 85 degrees.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:22.883
So tropical.

00:07:23.959 --> 00:07:27.300
And so like after we've
eaten and it's 85 degrees

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:28.930
and you're sitting shoulder-to-shoulder

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.680
in this like carpeted living room

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.780
with like the kerosene heater on,

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.880
like everybody just started wilting man.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.570
And then a couple of family
members would have to go outside

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.130
to smoke and then they come back in

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.920
and like, me and Christy
are really sensitive

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:43.890
to the smell of smoke.

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:47.110
And like, we got headaches
from just the residual smell

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:47.943
on the clothes.

00:07:47.943 --> 00:07:51.830
And we started to really
have a difficult time.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:53.270
So we had to, I was like,

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:56.860
Well, mom and Louis had to
go to Louis' family's thing.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:00.230
And then we were gonna
stay like two more hours.

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:03.640
I'm like after another
hour and the dog shows over

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:04.473
and stuff, I'm like-

00:08:04.473 --> 00:08:05.306
The dog show?

00:08:05.306 --> 00:08:07.890
We were wilting, we had
to like get out of there.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.710
So I'm like, well, and we had seen nanny

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.840
another day before, so we
got some good quality time.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.730
And then I was like, we said
our goodbyes and headed out.

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:18.357
And then we get out to the
car and Christy is like,

00:08:18.357 --> 00:08:20.360
"Well, we can't go back to Brittany's

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:23.370
because they're not there,
they're at JBs family's house.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.200
And we don't have a key

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.520
so that we don't have somewhere to be

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.950
for like two and a half hours."

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.740
I'm like, "Well, I really need a coffee."

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.360
And it's Thanksgiving day

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.950
and we're driving around to "McDonald's"

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:38.700
and then I get into "McDonald's"
and I'm like, "Yes."

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.009
And then the door at
"McDonald's" is closed.

00:08:41.009 --> 00:08:43.670
And I look across I mean,

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:45.600
and then I'll look across the street

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.880
and they got like a gas.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.230
I can't get gas station coffee.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.180
So then we drive to Fuchs Way I'm like,

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:53.810
there's a "McDonald's" in Fuchs Way.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:56.210
There's a Starbucks in Fuchs Way.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:59.240
I go to both of those,
they're both closed.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.240
I'm getting desperate.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.290
I go to the 'Dunkin' Donuts" closed.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.680
I'm starting to feel
like a real loser now.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:07.300
I'm driving my family
around on Thanksgiving day.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:09.830
Just feening for coffee

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:12.617
and getting turned away like "Les Mis."

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:15.300
Well, okay that's a little...

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.133
Okay.

00:09:16.133 --> 00:09:17.450
I don't remember this story "Les Mis."

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:18.430
I didn't watch the movie.

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:19.680
It's exactly like that.

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:23.740
Christy's like, there's a Sheetz.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.317
I'm like, "Gas station coffee?"

00:09:25.317 --> 00:09:27.410
Sheetz is not a gas station.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:28.440
Well, you know what?

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.700
You know what? I have
never been in a Sheetz.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:32.960
I've never been inside of one.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.167
What?

00:09:34.167 --> 00:09:35.000
I've heard about them.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.848
You can order food from the gas park.

00:09:36.848 --> 00:09:41.810
You can order lattes from a
screen inside, which I did.

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:43.060
Of course you did.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.520
And that explains why on Christmas day,

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.240
me and my immediate
family were playing cards

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:51.740
for an hour and a half
in a Sheetz gas station

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.880
because we had nowhere to be.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.810
That's why I got a group
text of you playing cards

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:02.840
in a gas station on Thanksgiving.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:05.410
And you know, I felt kind of defeated.

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:07.887
I'm like, "Is this a father fail?"

00:10:09.495 --> 00:10:12.550
Does this answer the question
at the top of the show that

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:17.010
like, it's better to not be
with family on Thanksgiving?

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:21.330
'Cause here I am choosing to
play cards just with my kids

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:25.980
and my wife in a Sheetz gas station.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:30.460
And then Christy's like, "You know what?

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:32.060
This could be a tradition."

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:32.893
Oh gosh.

00:10:32.893 --> 00:10:35.490
And it's a magical phrase.

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:38.880
No matter what happens,
if it's bad, try this.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:41.940
Next time you're in like a weird defeated

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:46.330
or odd holiday situation just
don't even think about it.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:47.390
Just say it out loud.

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:51.048
Speaking into the ether.
This could be a tradition.

00:10:51.048 --> 00:10:54.100
Everything changed at that moment.

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:56.586
Like I got a tingle on my spine.

00:10:56.586 --> 00:10:58.450
A Sheetz tingle.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:10:59.915
I got a Sheetz tingle.

00:10:59.915 --> 00:11:04.610
I'm like, "Yeah, even if we're
not here for Thanksgiving,

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.013
we can fly all the way
here to go to this Sheetz."

00:11:07.013 --> 00:11:08.978
And it's like, "No, we didn't mean that."

00:11:08.978 --> 00:11:11.510
But it changed the perspective
on the whole thing.

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:13.996
A sponsorship from
Sheetz would definitely-

00:11:13.996 --> 00:11:14.920
Work it.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.890
Change it.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:18.880
So that was my Thanksgiving.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:20.640
And by the way, then I left there

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.400
and we met Britton and his
family at a Waffle House.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:24.810
So I went from a Sheetz

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.270
and then spent the next
two and a half hours

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.720
at a Waffle House on Thanksgiving nights.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:30.660
That's an upgrade.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:35.230
Both places are great, but
Waffle House, that's prime.

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:36.980
We walked into the Waffle House

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:41.980
and the manager looked at
us and he said out loud,

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.567
basically at the top of his voice,

00:11:45.567 --> 00:11:47.487
"What the hell?"

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:52.010
And I thought it was like,
"Yeah, Thanksgiving night,

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:53.570
I know we're here at a Waffle House

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:55.030
course so are you manager?

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:57.550
It's great that you're here
so that I can be here."

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:59.847
And then it turned out
at the end he was a fan,

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:04.120
but he didn't tell me that until
two and a half hours later.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.150
So yeah, gas station in a Waffle House

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:09.300
on Thanksgiving night,
it can be a tradition.

